From 23d6a15dbe003190152c2fa35dbadbc0bf967414 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 16:11:34 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] drm: Offer DisplayPort's frame sequential stereo as an
 output filter

DisplayPort's own 3D is frame sequential: the source sends one eye per
frame of the mode's 2D timing and tells the display which eye each frame
carries (the Main Stream Attribute's MISC1 bits 2:1, DisplayPort 1.1a). The
edition's kernel has a mode flag for it, DRM_MODE_FLAG_3D_FRAME_SEQUENTIAL
(9 << 14, kernel series 36; defined here until libdrm has it), whose frame
is the two eyes side by side, left eye first, twice the mode's width: the
full side by side strip KWin already builds for its software frame
sequential twin. The kernel alternates the halves, signals the eye and
lands page flips only at the start of a left-eye frame, and it takes the
layout only on connectors whose driver opted in.

- A mode the display declares (an EDID detailed timing, a DisplayID stereo
  block) comes from the kernel with the flag and is listed as one of the
  display's 3D modes, Stereo3DSequentialDisplayPort; until now KWin left
  out a 3D flag it did not know.
- Elsewhere each 2D mode at 100 Hz or more gets a twin under "Other stereo
  formats", where a TEST_ONLY commit of the mode with the flag succeeds:
  DrmGpu::probeFrameSequential() tests each such mode once per mode list,
  on the connector's CRTC or a free one, with a dumb buffer as the primary
  plane covering the whole frame, and changes nothing. The twin is a mode of
  its own (its blob carries the flag), not the 2D mode KWin packs into; it
  is left out where the display declares the same timing.
- The frame is committed whole, as for side by side (full), and the eyes
  are composed into its halves with the output's eye swap, as for every 3D
  mode. There is no right-first twin: the eye order is signalled.
- Pacing: a flip's event comes one or two refreshes after the commit and
  only at a pair boundary, so the commit thread and the render loop run at
  half the refresh (stereoPresentationRate()), presentation stays vsync,
  and nothing assumes an event per eye; the software twin's scheduler is
  not involved.
- The output device interface sends the new mode flag from version 30.

The software frame sequential twins stay as their own options, and
neither kind is ever chosen automatically.

testMockDrmDisplayPortSequential covers the flag mapping and the frame,
the declared mode, the twin and its gating, the TEST_ONLY probe on a
connector with and without the opt-in (the mock refuses the layout where
the connector did not opt in, as the kernel core does), and the scanout of
the whole frame with the flagged mode.

